@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

@cindex configuration language
@cindex .cgra files
The @file{.cgra} language is INI-flavoured: a sequence of named sections, each
holding @code{key = value} directives.  A @samp{#} begins a trailing comment;
a line whose first non-blank character is @samp{;} is a whole-line comment.
Every entity is merged @emph{by name} across all loaded files, so a later file
overrides individual fields of an entity defined earlier.  The terse reference
is @command{man cgra 5}; this chapter is the tutorial.

@section Section types
@cindex sections
A section header is @code{[TYPE NAME]}.  Four types exist.

@subsection @code{[device NAME]}
@cindex device profile
A connection profile.

@table @code
@item port
serial path (e.g.@: @file{/dev/ttyUSB1}) or @samp{sim:} for the emulator.
@item baud
baud rate (default 115200).
@item timeout
per-reply timeout in milliseconds.
@item board
informational board tag (@code{basys3}, @code{nexys_a7}).
@end table

@subsection @code{[mode NAME]}
@cindex mode
An array configuration.  A mode either selects a @dfn{pattern} that generates
the sixteen PE words, or places PEs by hand.

@table @code
@item doc
one-line description (shown by @command{cgra modes}).
@item pattern
@code{diagonal} (default), @code{reduce}, @code{custom}, @code{systolic},
@code{conv}, or @code{scan}.  @xref{Dataflow}.
@item op
the ALU opcode for @code{diagonal}/@code{reduce}: @code{add sub mul mac and or
xor shl shr max min abs acc const} (and @code{nop}, @code{pass}).
@item a
@itemx b
operand sources: @code{north west self zero} or @code{const(K)} (a 16-bit
immediate).  Defaults: @code{a = north}, @code{b = west}.
@item steps
@code{rows} (= 4), @code{auto}, or an integer.
@item out
output tap: @code{diag} (the four diagonal PEs) or @code{pe R,C} (a single PE).
@item reset
@code{each} or @code{once}---whether to clear PE registers between input chunks.
@item pe R,C = op=@dots{} a=@dots{} b=@dots{}
for @code{pattern = custom}, place one PE (@math{0 \le R,C \le 3}).  Unlisted
PEs are @code{nop}.
@end table

@subsection @code{[pipeline NAME]}
@cindex pipeline
A chain of modes; the host feeds each stage's output into the next.

@table @code
@item doc
description.
@item stage = MODE [imm=K]
one stage.  Repeat for each stage; @code{imm=K} sets that stage's immediate.
The first stage may be binary (consume @var{a} and @var{b}); later stages are
unary.
@end table

@subsection @code{[io NAME]}
@cindex io profile
A data-binding profile for output.

@table @code
@item format
@code{dec}, @code{hex}, @code{bin} or @code{json}.
@item width
datapath width (informational; 16).
@item sep
@code{ws}, @code{comma} or @code{newline}.
@end table

@section Includes and variables
@cindex include
@cindex set
@cindex variables
A file may pull in another with @code{include "other.cgra"}.  The include is
searched, in order: relative to the including file, each @env{CGRA_PATH} entry,
the user config dir and its @file{stdlib/}, then the system share and
@file{/etc/cgra}.

@code{set NAME = value} defines a variable; @code{$NAME} and @code{$@{NAME@}}
expand it in any later value.  For example:

@example
set GAIN = 7
[mode amplify]
pattern = diagonal
op      = mul
a       = north
b       = const($GAIN)
@end example

@section Discovery and precedence
@cindex discovery
@cindex precedence
Configuration is loaded from several roots, lowest to highest precedence:

@enumerate
@item the built-in defaults (compiled in);
@item @file{/etc/cgra};
@item the user directory (@env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}@file{/cgra} or
      @file{~/.config/cgra});
@item each @env{CGRA_PATH} directory (colon-separated, like @env{PATH});
@item project-local: @env{CGRA_CONFIG} if set, else @file{./.cgra/};
@item the @option{-c} file (highest).
@end enumerate

@cindex conf.d
Within each directory, @file{config.cgra} loads first, then the remaining
@file{*.cgra} alphabetically, then a @file{conf.d/} drop-in directory
alphabetically.  So @file{conf.d/99_overrides.cgra} beats
@file{conf.d/01_base.cgra} by name---the Nginx/systemd convention.
@command{cgra config} prints the resolved paths.
